  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThere is a mid-level trough over west central Texas (via SPC \nMesoanalysis) that is expected to gradually move northeastward over \nthe southern Plains today/tonight. Additionally, a stout 100+ knot \nupper jet is located over southeastern Ontario, placing northern \nIllinois in the right entrance region of its location and therefore \nin an area increased upper level divergence. For know, a near \nsurface dry airmass has been winning the day keeping all activity on \nKLOT radar currently showing up in the western portions of the \nforecast area to mainly virga, to maybe a few sprinkles. As a plume \nof increased moisture and warm air advection moves into the area \nthis evening and in the overnight, there is a better chance for \nincreased coverage for light showers into Saturday morning. Given \nthe unfavorable time of day, thunder is not expected. Although \nslightly steepening low/mid-level lapse rates keeps the chances non-\nzero, but below formal forecast mention.\n\nAs the upper level jet streak weakens and moves east tomorrow \nmorning, the mid/upper level trough is expected to move over the \nwestern Great Lakes Saturday afternoon and evening. With weaker \nforcing and a lingering dry boundary layer air mass, confidence is \nlower for precipitation tomorrow evening. Slight chance PoPs were \nmaintained for tomorrow night for the isolated chance for a light \nshower, if not just sprinkles/drizzle during any trick-or-treating. \nCertainly not any kind of washout, some areas will probably remain \ncompletely dry. But enough of a nuisance to keep precip mention in \nthe forecast through Sunday morning.\n\nA pseudo-stationary upper level cutoff low west of Seattle, WA is \nprojected to finally move eastward over the intermountain west on \nSunday as another short wave currently near Baja California moves \nnorthward, merging with the low, further amplifying the trough early \nnext week. While specific details still need to be ironed out, \nmodels are showing fairly good agreement that this trough will \ninduce surface cyclogenesis east of the Rockies and that will move \nover the Great Lakes in the middle of next week bringing better chances  \nfor widespread rain. Behind this system, a cooler airmass will drop \ntemperatures down to make it feel a little more autumn-like.\n\nDK",
